On 04/20/2017 09:20 AM, Bjorn Helgaas wrote:
> On Wed, Apr 19, 2017 at 7:25 PM, Jon Masters <jcm@redhat.com> wrote:
>> One additional footnote. I spent a bunch of time recently on my personal
>> Xeon systems walking through the PCIe topology and aligning on how to
>> advise the ARM server community proceed going forward. If you look at
>> how Intel vs AMD handle their host bridges for example, you'll see two
>> very different approaches to the case of cross-socket PCIe.
> 
> As a learning opportunity for me, can you share "lspci -vv" examples
> that show this Intel vs AMD difference?

Attached intel_lspci_verbose (Intel(R) Xeon(R) CPU E5-2650 v4 @ 2.20GHz
and ryzen_lscpi_verbose (AMD Ryzen 7 1800X Eight-Core Processor). The
latter is a single socket, but I would expect dual socket Naples to look
different from the Intel case just based on what I have seen of the
single socket varieties. I'll dig up an older example 2S AMD box for
which I can publish numbers (needs to be a production one, etc.) - also,
waiting to get a Zen based server for home with baited breath.

In particular, you'll see that Intel do the split segment trick where
they have a low half of the segment containing the regular topology off
a single "bridge" representing the downstream from both sockets (their
RC has built-in glue so that it actually spans multi-socket over their
interconnect), and a high segment half where they shove all the on-chip
devices from both sockets. In other words, what you see is nothing like
reality. This is created to make life easy for Windows and so on. It
just thinks everything lives under one fairly flat topology.

Intel then define a single segment group number representing those two
halves in the MCFG with a single segment group number. Contrast this
with how (e.g.) another ARM server here does PCI in which it has 4
segment groups defined, with two per socket, splitting devices evenly
between the sockets based upon where they actually are located.

The industry seems to prefer (through muscle memory) the Intel approach
of appearing to be a single segment with all the overhead and hoops INTC
jump through to concoct that fiction. In my experience, the only reason
that matters is that unless folks see a single host bridge at the top
and the way Intel do it they say "hey, what's wrong with PCI...". It's
real good kool aid they've been drinking for years.

Jon.

